  ],
  "text": "A passing siren does not change its pitch. Not once. It sounds higher coming towards you and lower going away, and the siren is doing nothing different. The sound leaves as a steady series of pressure waves, evenly spaced. But the source is moving, so each new wave starts from slightly closer than the last one. That bunches them up ahead of it and stretches them out behind. And more waves arriving per second is exactly what a higher pitch is. So the pitch you hear is a property of the motion, not of the siren. The same trick reads a car's speed off a radar gun, and finds the rain inside a storm. And it works on light just as well as it works on sound. Which is how we know the whole universe is moving away from us. But that is for later. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
